    {
      "id": "evt_gcal_com_effective_altruism_nyc_a082a41c",
      "name": "AI Safety workshop",
      "type": "effective altruism",
      "locationId": "",
      "communityId": "com_effective_altruism_nyc",
      "description": "You are hosting this event. View the public page at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/url?q=https://luma.com/i9ak92r5&amp;sa=D&amp;source=calendar&amp;ust=1786204179764918&amp;usg=AOvVaw198TnzbY5ihREmiG6js-y4\" target=\"_blank\">https://luma.com/i9ak92r5</a>\n\nManage the event at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/url?q=https://luma.com/event/manage/evt-nsH79d9HIYDiXxu&amp;sa=D&amp;source=calendar&amp;ust=1786204179764918&amp;usg=AOvVaw0qiVPIOVMnb_mzvWxZ8rK0\" target=\"_blank\">https://luma.com/event/manage/evt-nsH79d9HIYDiXxu</a>\n\nAddress:\n26 Broadway\n3rd Floor, Primary Coworking\nNew York, NY\nUnited States\n\nNote we&#39;re other 3rd floor in the Primary co working space\nThis session we&#39;re digging into whether today&#39;s AI models are already quietly misaligned. \n\nThe format of the event is more of a reading group discussion than an interactive workshop. Please read the recommended reading to get the most out of it.\n\nOur main read is Ryan Greenblatt&#39;s &quot;Current AIs seem pretty misaligned to me,&quot; an AI safety researcher&#39;s first-hand case that frontier models routinely oversell their work, bury problems, quit early while claiming success, and cheat on tasks without admitting it. We&#39;ll pair it with OpenAI&#39;s\u2026\n\nHosted by Jonathan Calenzani",
